Subject: [PATCH] vhost: release queued work after saving the next node

The safe llist iterator saves work->node.next before executing the loop body
in C, but clear_bit() does not order that load. On AArch64 GCC 15.2 the
compiled worker clears VHOST_WORK_QUEUED before loading the next pointer.
A concurrent producer can then requeue the current node and overwrite its
next pointer, losing the rest of the detached batch. Lost work remains
marked queued and subsequent notifications cannot enqueue it again.

Use release semantics in both task and kthread workers. This orders the
iterator load before a producer can acquire the cleared bit and reuse the
node. It does not change the virtio feature or eventfd contracts.

The missing ordering was also discussed upstream:
https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2023/06/05/811

Locally reproduced by pausing the real AArch64 worker after its flag clear
and simulating one valid producer requeue. The original worker loses the
following TX work; with this patch the same interleaving executes both.
Physical RPi5 recovery has not yet been verified.

--- a/drivers/vhost/vhost.c
+++ b/drivers/vhost/vhost.c
@@ -422,7 +422,11 @@
 		/* make sure flag is seen after deletion */
 		smp_wmb();
 		llist_for_each_entry_safe(work, work_next, node, node) {
-			clear_bit(VHOST_WORK_QUEUED, &work->flags);
+			/* Requeueing may overwrite node.next as soon as the bit
+			 * clears. Order the iterator's load of work_next before
+			 * releasing the node to vhost_worker_queue().
+			 */
+			clear_bit_unlock(VHOST_WORK_QUEUED, &work->flags);
 			__set_current_state(TASK_RUNNING);
 			kcov_remote_start_common(worker->kcov_handle);
 			work->fn(work);
@@ -449,7 +453,11 @@
 		/* make sure flag is seen after deletion */
 		smp_wmb();
 		llist_for_each_entry_safe(work, work_next, node, node) {
-			clear_bit(VHOST_WORK_QUEUED, &work->flags);
+			/* Requeueing may overwrite node.next as soon as the bit
+			 * clears. Order the iterator's load of work_next before
+			 * releasing the node to vhost_worker_queue().
+			 */
+			clear_bit_unlock(VHOST_WORK_QUEUED, &work->flags);
 			kcov_remote_start_common(worker->kcov_handle);
 			work->fn(work);
 			kcov_remote_stop();
